  (header-case \"Foo fooF\") => \"Foo-Foo-F\"
  (header-case 'foo{foo.f) => \"Foo-Foo-F\"
  (header-case #f) => \"F\"
  (header-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "INSERT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(string/char index s)" :DOCSTRING "Insert the given string (or character) at the `index' into `s' and return a new string.

  If `index' is out of bounds, ignore and return `s'." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "JOIN" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(separator strings)" :DOCSTRING "Join all the strings of the list with a separator.

  `separator' can be a string or a character.

  Example:
  (str:join \",\" '(\"a\" \"b\" \"c\")
  => \"a,b,c\"" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "LETTERS?" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if `s' contains only letters (including unicode letters).

   (alphap \"éß\") ;; => nil
   (lettersp \"éß\") ;; => t" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "LETTERSNUMP" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if `s' contains only letters (including unicode letters) and digits." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "LETTERSP" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if `s' contains only letters (including unicode letters).

   (alphap \"éß\") ;; => nil
   (lettersp \"éß\") ;; => t" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "LINES" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s &key (omit-nulls *omit-nulls*))" :DOCSTRING "Split the string by newline characters and return a list of lines. A terminal newline character does NOT result in an extra empty string." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "MATCH" :KIND :MACRO :LAMBDA-LIST "(str &body match-branches)" :DOCSTRING "A COND-like macro to match substrings and bind variables to matches. Regular expressions are allowed for matches.

  _ is a placeholder that is ignored.

  THIS MACRO IS EXPERIMENTAL and might break in future releases.

  Example:

  (str:match \"a 1 b 2 d\"
    ((\"a \" x \" b \" y \" d\") ;; => matched
     (+ (parse-integer x) (parse-integer y)))
    (t
     'default-but-not-for-this-case)) ;; default branch
  ;; => 3

  (str:match \"a 1 b c d\"
    ((\"a 2 b\" _ \"d\") ;; => not matched
     (print \"pass\"))
    ((\"a \" _ \" b c d\") ;; => matched
     \"here we go\")
    (t 'default-but-not-for-this-case)) ;; default branch
  ;; => \"here we go\"

  Match with regexs:

  (str:match \"123 hello 456\"
    ((\"\\d+\" s \"\\d+\")
     s)
  (t \"nothing\"))
  ;; => \" hello \"
  " :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "NO-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s &key (replacement \" \"))" :DOCSTRING "Transform `s' to lower case space delimited. Use REPLACEMENT as delimiter.

  Examples:
  (no-case \"Foo fooF\" :replacement \",\") => \"foo,foo,f\"
  (no-case 'foo{foo.f) => \"foo foo f\"
  (no-case #F) => \"f\"
  (no-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "NON-BLANK-STRING-P" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if `s' is a string and is non blank (it doesn't exclusively contain whitespace characters).

  Like `(not (blankp s))', with a `stringp' check. Useful in context." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "NON-EMPTY-STRING-P" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if `s' is a string and is non-empty.

  Like `(not (emptyp s))', with a `stringp' check. Useful in context." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "NUMERIC?" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if `s' contains at least one character and all characters are numerical." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "NUMERICP" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if `s' contains at least one character and all characters are numerical." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PAD" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(len s &key (pad-side *pad-side*) (pad-char *pad-char*))" :DOCSTRING "Fill `s' with characters until it is of the given length. By default, add spaces on the right.

Filling with spaces can be done with format:

    (format nil \"~v@a\" len s) ;; with or without the @ directive

`pad-side': to pad `:right' (the default), `:left' or `:center'.
`pad-char': padding character (or string of one character). Defaults to a space." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PAD-CENTER" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(len s &key (pad-char *pad-char*))" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PAD-LEFT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(len s &key (pad-char *pad-char*))" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PAD-RIGHT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(len s &key (pad-char *pad-char*))" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PARAGRAPHS" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(string)" :DOCSTRING "Split the string by paragraphs. Paragraphs are separated by two new lines.
  Return a list of strings. Each paragraph has whitespace strimmed around it." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PARAM-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Transform `s' to param-case.

  Examples:
  (param-case \"Foo fooF\") => \"foo-foo-f\"
  (param-case (quote foo{foo.f)) => \"foo-foo-f\"
  (param-case #F) => \"f\"
  (param-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PASCAL-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Transform `s' to Pascal Case

  Examples:
  (pascal-case \"Foo fooF\") => \"FooFooF\"
  (pascal-case :foo{foo.f) => \"FooFooF\"
  (pascal-case #f) => \"F\"
  (pascal-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PATH-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Transform `s' to path/case

  Examples:
  (path-case \"Foo fooF\") => \"foo/foo/f\"
  (path-case :foo{foo.f) => \"foo/foo/f\"
  (path-case #F) => \"f\"
  (path-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PREFIX" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(items)" :DOCSTRING "Find the common prefix between strings.

   Uses the built-in `mismatch', that returns the position at which
   the strings fail to match.

   Example: `(str:prefix '(\"foobar\" \"foozz\"))` => \"foo\"

   - items: list of strings
   - Return: a string.

  " :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PREFIX?" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(items prefix)" :DOCSTRING "Return PREFIX if all ITEMS start with it." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PREFIXP" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(items prefix)" :DOCSTRING "Return PREFIX if all ITEMS start with it." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "PRUNE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(len s &key (ellipsis *ellipsis*))" :DOCSTRING "If s is longer than `len', truncate it to this length and add the `*ellipsis*' at the end (\"...\" by default). Cut it down to `len' minus the length of the ellipsis." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "REMOVE-PUNCTUATION" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s &key (replacement \" \"))" :DOCSTRING "Remove the punctuation characters from `s', replace them with `replacement' (defaults to a space) and strip continuous whitespace." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "REPEAT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(count string)" :DOCSTRING "Make a string of S repeated COUNT times." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "REPLACE-ALL" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(old new s &key regex)" :DOCSTRING "Replace all occurences of `old' by `new' in `s'.

  By default, metacharacters are treated as normal characters.
  If `regex' is not nil, `old' is treated as a regular expression.

  Examples:
  (replace-all \"+\" \"'\\'\" \"foo+bar\") ;; => \"foo'\\'bar\"
  (replace-all \"fo+\" \"frob\" \"foofoo bar\" :regex t) ;; => \"frobfrob bar\"" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "REPLACE-FIRST" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(old new s &key regex)" :DOCSTRING "Replace the first occurence of `old` by `new` in `s`.

  By default, metacharacters are treated as normal characters.
  If `regex' is not nil, `old' is treated as a regular expression.

  Examples:
  (replace-first \"aa\" \"oo\" \"faaaa\") => \"fooaa\"
  (replace-first \"fo+\" \"frob\" \"foofoo bar\" :regex t) => \"frobfoo bar\"" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "REPLACE-USING" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(replacement-list s &key regex)" :DOCSTRING "Replace all associations given by pairs in a list and return a new string.

  The `replacement-list' alternates a string to replace (case sensitive) and its replacement.
  By default, metacharacters in the string to replace are treated as normal characters.
  If `regex' is not nil, strings to replace are treated as regular expressions.

  Example:
  (replace-using (list \"{{phone}}\" \"987\")
                 \"call {{phone}}\")
  => \"call 987\"

  (replace-using (list \"fo+\" \"frob\"
                       \"ba+\" \"Bob\")
                 \"foo bar\"
                 :regex t)
  => \"frob Bobr\"

  It calls `replace-all' as many times as there are replacements to do." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "RSPLIT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(sep s &key (omit-nulls *omit-nulls*) limit regex)" :DOCSTRING "Similar to `split', except we split from the end. In particular,
the results will be be different when `limit` is provided.

   If `regex' is not `nil`, `separator' is treated as a regular expression." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "S-ASSOC-VALUE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(alist key)" :DOCSTRING "Return the value of a cons cell in `alist' with key `key', tested
with `string-equal' (case-insensitive).
  The second return value is the cons cell." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "S-FIRST" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return the first substring of `s'." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "S-LAST" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return the last substring of `s'." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "S-MEMBER" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(list s &key (test (function string=)) (ignore-case *ignore-case*))" :DOCSTRING "Return T if `s' is a member of `list'. Do not ignore case by default.

  NOTE: S-MEMBER's arguments' order is the reverse of CL:MEMBER.

  If `:ignore-case' or `*ignore-case*' are not nil, ignore case (using `string-equal' instead of `string=').

  Unlike CL:MEMBER, S-MEMBER returns T or NIL, instead of the tail of LIST whose first element satisfies the test." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "S-NTH" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(n s)" :DOCSTRING "Return the nth substring of `s'.

   You could also use
   (string (elt \"test\" 1))
   ;; => \"e\"" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "S-REST" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return the rest substring of `s'." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SENTENCE-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Transform `s' to Sentence case

  Examples:
  (sentence-case \"Foo.fooF\") => \"Foo foo f\"
  (sentence-case (quote foo{foo.f)) => \"Foo foo f\"
  (sentence-case #f) => \"F\"
  (sentence-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SHORTEN" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(len s &key (ellipsis *ellipsis*))" :DOCSTRING "If s is longer than `len', truncate it to this length and add the `*ellipsis*' at the end (\"...\" by default). Cut it down to `len' minus the length of the ellipsis." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SNAKE-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Transform `s' to snake_case

  Examples:
  (snake-case \"Foo fooF\") => \"foo_foo_f\"
  (snake-case 'foo{foo.f) => \"foo_foo_f\"
  (snake-case #f) => \"f\"
  (snake-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SPLIT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(separator s &key (omit-nulls *omit-nulls*) limit (start 0) end regex)" :DOCSTRING "Split s into substring by separator (cl-ppcre takes a regex, we do not).

   `limit' limits the number of elements returned (i.e. the string is
   split at most `limit' - 1 times).
   If `regex' is not nil, `separator' is treated as a regular expression.

   Examples:
   (str:split \",\" \"foo,bar\") ;; => (\"foo\" \"bar\")
   (str:split \"[,|;]\" \"foo,bar;baz\" :regex t) ;; => (\"foo\" \"bar\" \"baz\")
" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SPLIT-OMIT-NULLS" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(separator s &key regex)" :DOCSTRING "Call `split' with :omit-nulls to t.

   Can be clearer in certain situations.

   If `regex' is not nil, `separator' is treated as a regular expression." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "STARTS-WITH-P" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(start s &key (ignore-case *ignore-case*))" :DOCSTRING "Return t if S starts with the substring `START', nil otherwise.

  START can be a string or a character." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "STARTS-WITH?" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(start s &key (ignore-case *ignore-case*))" :DOCSTRING "Return t if S starts with the substring `START', nil otherwise.

  START can be a string or a character." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "STRING-CASE" :KIND :MACRO :LAMBDA-LIST "(str &body forms)" :DOCSTRING "A case-like macro that works with strings (case works only with symbols).
  You can either supply single-item clauses, multiple-items clauses, or otherwise.

  Example:

  (str:string-case input
    (\"foo\" (do something))
    ((\"hello\" \"test\") 5)
    (nil (print \"input is nil\")
    (otherwise (print \"none of the previous forms was caught\")))

  You might also like pattern matching with `str:match'.

  Note that there is also http://quickdocs.org/string-case/.
  " :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SUBSTRING" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(start end s)" :DOCSTRING "Return the substring of `s' from `start' to `end'.

It uses `subseq' with differences:
- argument order, s at the end
- `start' and `end' can be lower than 0 or bigger than the length of s.
- for convenience `end' can be nil or t to denote the end of the string.
" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SUFFIX" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(items)" :DOCSTRING "Find the common suffix between strings.

   Uses the built-in `mismatch', that returns the position at which
   the strings fail to match.

   Example: `(str:suffix '(\"foobar\" \"zzbar\"))` => \"bar\"

   - items: list of strings
   - Return: a string.

  " :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SUFFIX?" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(items suffix)" :DOCSTRING "Return `suffix' if all items end with it.
  Otherwise, return nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SUFFIXP" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(items suffix)" :DOCSTRING "Return `suffix' if all items end with it.
  Otherwise, return nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "SWAP-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Reverse case for each character in `s'.

  Examples:
  (swap-case \"Foo fooF\") => \"fOO FOOf\"
  (swap-case :FOO{FOO.F) => \"foo{foo.f\"
  (swap-case #f) => \"F\"
  (swap-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "TITLE-CASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Transform `s' to Title Case

  Examples:
  (title-case \"Foo fooF\") => \"Foo Foo F\"
  (title-case :foo{foo.f) => \"Foo Foo F\"
  (title-case #f) => \"F\"
  (title-case nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "TO-FILE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(pathname s &key (if-exists :supersede) (if-does-not-exist :create))" :DOCSTRING "Write string `s' to file `pathname'. If the file does not exist, create it (use `:if-does-not-exist'), if it already exists, replace its content (`:if-exists').

Returns the string written to file." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "TRIM" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s &key (char-bag *whitespaces*))" :DOCSTRING "Removes all characters in `char-bag` (default: whitespaces) at the beginning and end of `s`.
   If supplied, char-bag has to be a sequence (e.g. string or list of characters).

   Examples: (trim \"  foo \") => \"foo\"
             (trim \"+-*foo-bar*-+\" :char-bag \"+-*\") => \"foo-bar\"
             (trim \"afood\" :char-bag (str:concat \"a\" \"d\")) => \"foo\"" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "TRIM-LEFT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s &key (char-bag *whitespaces*))" :DOCSTRING "Removes all characters in `char-bag` (default: whitespaces) at the beginning of `s`.
   If supplied, char-bag has to be a sequence (e.g. string or list of characters).

   Examples: (trim-left \"  foo \") => \"foo \"
             (trim-right \"+-*foo-bar*-+\" :char-bag \"+-*\") => \"foo-bar*-+\"
             (trim-left \"afood\" :char-bag (list #a #d) ;; => \"food\"" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "TRIM-RIGHT" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s &key (char-bag *whitespaces*))" :DOCSTRING "Removes all characters in `char-bag` (default: whitespaces) at the end of `s`.
   If supplied, char-bag has to be a sequence (e.g. string or list of characters).

   Examples: (trim-right \"  foo \") => \"  foo\"
             (trim-right \"+-*foo-bar*-+\" :char-bag \"+-*\") => \"+-*foo-bar\"
             (trim-right \"afood\" :char-bag (list #a #d) ;; => \"afoo\"" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "UNLINES" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(strings)" :DOCSTRING "Join the list of strings with a newline character." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "UNPARAGRAPHS" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(strings)" :DOCSTRING "Join the list of strings by two newlines (have them separated by a blank line)." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "UNWORDS" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(strings)" :DOCSTRING "Join the list of strings with a whitespace." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "UPCASE" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return the uppercase version of `s'.
  Call the built-in `string-upcase', but return nil if `s' is
  nil (instead of the string \"NIL\").

  Examples:
  (upcase \"Foo fooF\") => \"FOO FOOF\"
  (upcase :foo{foo.f) => \"FOO{FOO.F\"
  (upcase #f) => \"F\"
  (upcase nil) => nil" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "UPCASE?" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if all alphabetical characters of `s' are uppercase." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "UPCASEP" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s)" :DOCSTRING "Return t if all alphabetical characters of `s' are uppercase." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "VERSION" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "()" :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "WORDS" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(s &key (limit 0))" :DOCSTRING "Return list of words, which were delimited by white space. If the optional limit is 0 (the default), trailing empty strings are removed from the result list (see cl-ppcre)." :ORIGIN "str")
  (:NAME "WRAPPED-IN-P" :KIND :FUNCTION :LAMBDA-LIST "(start/end s)" :DOCSTRING "Does S start and end with `START/END'?
If true, return S. Otherwise, return nil.

Example:

    (str:wrapped-in-p \"/\" \"/foo/\"  ;; => \"/foo/\"
    (str:wrapped-in-p \"/\" \"/foo\"  ;; => nil

See also: UIOP:STRING-ENCLOSED-P (prefix s suffix).
" :ORIGIN "str"))
 )
